{# polysource/easyadmin-filter-bridge — subpanel-mode index template.

   Opt-in via:

       public function configureCrud(Crud $crud): Crud
       {
           return $crud->overrideTemplate(
               'crud/index',
               '@PolysourceEasyAdminFilterBridge/crud/index_subpanel.html.twig',
           );
       }

   Behaviour:
   - Inherits the chips bar override (`crud/index.html.twig` via the
     `@PolysourceEasyAdminFilterBridge` namespace).
   - Adds the `polysource-filter-subpanel` body class so the
     subpanel section of the bundle stylesheet (shipped in
     `Resources/public/polysource-filter-bridge.css`, linked by the
     parent template) restyles EA's `#modal-filters` as a
     right-anchored offcanvas-style panel instead of a centered
     modal. Panel width is themable via `--polysource-subpanel-width`
     (+ `-tablet`).
   - EA's AJAX form-loading flow stays intact: the EA JS still
     fetches `?action=renderFilters` and injects HTML into
     `.modal-body`. We only change the modal's positioning + slide-in
     animation via CSS — Bootstrap's modal lifecycle (focus trap,
     ESC, backdrop) is preserved.

   Why CSS over JS:
   - Replacing EA's modal with a Bootstrap offcanvas would mean
     re-implementing EA's filter-form AJAX loader, the
     apply/clear button wiring, and the autocomplete/toggle
     handlers — all of which target `.modal-body` and `.modal`
     selectors. Restyling via CSS keeps EA's JS happy and ships in
     ~50 lines.
   - Hosts who want a "real" offcanvas (different focus management,
     different transition) can override this template with their
     own.
#}
{% extends '@PolysourceEasyAdminFilterBridge/crud/index.html.twig' %}

{% block body_class %}{{ parent() }} polysource-filter-subpanel{% endblock %}
